      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-222194",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $A\u2229(B\u222aC)\u2286(A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x \u2208 A\u2229(B\u222aC)$.\n> $L2$: Then, $x \u2208 A$ and $x \u2208 B\u222aC$.\n> $L3$: **Case 1:** If $x \u2208 B$, then $x \u2208 Aandx \u2208 B$\n> $L4$: $\u21d2x \u2208 A\u2229B$\n> $L5$: $\u21d2x \u2208 (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n> $L6$: **Case 2:** Similarly, if $x \u2208 C$, then $x \u2208 A\u2229C\u21d2x \u2208 (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n> $L7$: In both cases, $x \u2208 (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n> $L8$: Therefore, $A\u2229(B\u222aC)\u2286(A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}\n\nLine $L5$ follows from line $L4$ due to the fact that {{field-2}} for any sets $X$ and $Y$\n\nSeparating the proof into case 1 and case 2 was necessary since {{field-3}}"
